              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "In a methane ($CH_4$) molecule, the chemical bonds are **single covalent bonds** (specifically, **$\\sigma$ (sigma) bonds**) formed between one central carbon atom and four surrounding hydrogen atoms. \n\nHere is a detailed breakdown of the characteristics, structure, and physics of the bonds in methane:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Hybridization and Orbital Overlap\n* **Carbon's Hybridization:** To form four equivalent bonds, the carbon atom undergoes **$sp^3$ hybridization**. Carbon's valence shell has one $2s$ orbital and three $2p$ orbitals. These mix to create four identical $sp^3$ hybrid orbitals, each containing one unpaired electron.\n* **Orbital Overlap:** Each C\u2013H bond is formed by the head-on overlap of one of Carbon's $sp^3$ hybrid orbitals with the $1s$ orbital of a Hydrogen atom. This head-on overlap creates a strong **$\\sigma$ (sigma) bond**.\n\n### 2. Geometry and Bond Angles\n* **Molecular Geometry:** Because of Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion (VSEPR) theory, the four bonding pairs of electrons repel each other equally and push as far apart as possible. This results in a **tetrahedral geometry**.\n* **Bond Angle:** The H\u2013C\u2013H bond angle is exactly **$109.5^\\circ$** (more precisely, $109.47^\\circ$).\n\n### 3. Bond Length and Strength\n* **Bond Length:** The distance between the nucleus of the carbon atom and each hydrogen atom is identical, measuring **$108.7 \\text{ pm}$** (picometers) or $1.09 \\text{ \u00c5}$ (angstroms).\n* **Bond Dissociation Energy (BDE):** \n  * The energy required to break the *first* C\u2013H bond (homolytic cleavage) is approximately **$439 \\text{ kJ/mol}$** ($104 \\text{ kcal/mol}$).\n  * The **average** C\u2013H bond energy (the average energy required to break all four bonds one by one) is about **$413 \\text{ to } 416 \\text{ kJ/mol}$**. Methane has one of the strongest $sp^3$ C\u2013H bonds in organic chemistry.\n\n### 4. Polarity\n* **Bond Polarity:** Carbon has an electronegativity of 2.5, while hydrogen has an electronegativity of 2.1. The difference (0.4) is very small, meaning the C\u2013H bonds are considered **weakly polar covalent** (essentially nonpolar).\n* **Molecular Polarity:** Because methane is perfectly symmetrical (tetrahedral), any slight pull of electrons toward the carbon atom from the hydrogen atoms cancels out in three dimensions. Therefore, the methane molecule as a whole is **completely nonpolar**, making it insoluble in water but highly soluble in nonpolar solvents.",
